From dedbc55637cc43a859c45bbdf03c0507655f6d12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Eduardo=20Garc=C3=ADa-M=C3=A1dico=20Portabella?=
 <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 17 Jul 2026 20:44:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 055/106] WIP: load captured RX volume calibration into the DSP
 (still silent)

Adds a q6mvm/q6cvp driver path that maps the captured 4316-byte vocvol
table (calibrations/vocvol_handset_rx7_tx4_fs0.bin) into DSP-visible
memory via VSS_IMEMORY_CMD_MAP_PHYSICAL and registers it with
VSS_IVOCPROC_CMD_REGISTER_VOL_CALIBRATION_DATA, so
VSS_IVOLUME_CMD_SET_STEP has a table to resolve against.

Real-hardware findings baked into this version, each cost a boot cycle:
- mvm's apr_device never goes through of_dma_configure() and rejects
  dma_set_mask_and_coherent() outright (-EIO) on this platform; made
  non-fatal in q6mvm_probe(), and the actual DMA allocations now use
  q6voice's own platform device instead.
- is_cached=1 (copied from the downstream ion-backed allocation) is
  wrong for our dma_alloc_coherent() buffer and caused a real ADSP
  firmware crash (remoteproc fatal error, full system hang, hard
  reboot required) - fixed to is_cached=0.
- VSS_IMEMORY_CMD_MAP_PHYSICAL then failed cleanly with error 2 (bad
  param, no crash) until both DMA allocations were rounded up to a
  full page - the table/mapped-address alignment requirement in the
  downstream header isn't guaranteed by dma_alloc_coherent() for
  sub-page requests.

With all of the above fixed, the whole call sequence (session create/
enable/attach, vol cal register, volume step, mute, start) completes
with zero errors for the first time - but the call is still completely
silent. Two follow-up hypotheses were checked and ruled out with
evidence, not just reasoning:
- Missing CVP_VOCPROC_STATIC_CAL_TYPE / CVS_VOCSTRM_STATIC_CAL_TYPE
  registration: exhaustive harness captures across every acdb_loader
  entry point show cal_type 2/4/5/6/7 are never sent by this device's
  vendor library at all, and downstream's own call sites don't check
  these commands' return values either - not required for the
  built-in topologies in use here.
- VSID mismatch with the modem: downstream's VSID constants
  (VOICE_SESSION_VSID etc.) are fixed per call-type values, not
  per-call negotiated with the modem - our session-name-based pairing
  is already the equivalent mechanism.

Root cause of the remaining silence is still unknown. Not yet verified
working - do not generate a numbered patch/APKBUILD entry for this
until it actually produces audio.
